content.text:
OK, here's what's going on:

**Error 1 — `chartAnalysis.ts` lines 155-161 (`getFixedStarsWithinLongitude`):**

The `.map()` produces `(string | number)[][]` — TypeScript doesn't infer tuple types from `.map` results, so it sees each inner array as `(string | number)[]` rather than `[string, number]`. Then `.filter` makes it worse because the destructured `starName` is `string | number`, hence `distance < maximumDistance` fails too (comparing `string | number` to `number`).

The simplest fix: rewrite it as a procedural loop. Build the map imperatively:

```ts
const result = new Map<string, number>();
for (const [starName, longitude] of fixedStarPositions.getPositions()) {
    const distance = angleShortDistance(lon, longitude);
    if (distance < maximumDistance) {
        result.set(starName, distance);
    }
}
return result;
```

Alternatively, if you want to keep the functional style, you could add `as [string, number]` to the `.map` return, but honestly the procedural version is clearer here.

**Error 2 — `ChartSummary.tsx` line 105 (the `ref` callback):**

The `ref` callback `(el) => nodeRefs.current.set(node, el)` returns the `Map` (since `.set()` returns the map). React 19 ref callbacks support cleanup functions (returning a function means "run this on unmount"), so TypeScript now rejects returning a non-void/non-function value. The fix is just to add `void`:

```ts
ref={(el) => { nodeRefs.current.set(node, el); }}
```

Wrapping the body in braces makes it a statement (no implicit return), so it returns `undefined`.

