I think the cleanest solution is: have the compute function return the nodes to *delete* separately from the nodes to *set*. Instead of smuggling deletion semantics through `undefined` values in a `Map<Node, number>`, return an object with two fields:

```ts
type PositionUpdate = {
    set: Map<Node, number>;
    remove: Node[];
};
```

When HCP is null, you return `{ set: new Map(), remove: [Node.PART_OF_WEALTH, Node.PART_OF_DEATH] }`. When HCP is present, you return `{ set: new Map([[Node.PART_OF_WEALTH, ...], [Node.PART_OF_DEATH, ...]]), remove: [] }`.

Then in the change methods, after spreading the `set` entries into the new positions map, you just delete the `remove` entries:

```ts
const update = computeHighDependencyArabicPartPositions(...);
const newPositions = new Map([...this._positions, ...update.set]);
for (const node of update.remove) newPositions.delete(node);
```

This is self-documenting — the return type literally says "here's what to set, here's what to remove." No sentinel values, no undefined-as-meaning, no guards in the constructor. The deletion logic lives right next to the spreading logic in the change methods, which is where it belongs. And the compute functions stay honest about what they're doing.

I think that's fine. A named type with a comment makes the semantics explicit, and the filter function keeps the messiness contained to one place:

```ts
/** Values of null indicate that the node should be removed from positions. */
type PositionPatch = Map<Node, number | null>;

function applyPatch(positions: Map<Node, number>, patch: PositionPatch): Map<Node, number> {
	const result = new Map(positions);
	for (const [node, value] of patch) {
		if (value === null) result.delete(node);
		else result.set(node, value);
	}
	return result;
}
```

Then all the change methods just do `applyPatch(this._positions, computeWhatever(...))`. The null semantics are documented once, handled once, and everything downstream stays `Map<Node, number>`. Honestly more succinct than either the `set/remove` approach or the raw spread pattern you have now.

